Now, before finalizing, reconsider Issue A severity/validity once more. The load-more flow: click → loadMore(next) → cursors [null, N] → render → useQueries adds query for cursor N → loadingMore true → button disabled. Double-click: two click events. React processes each synchronously (discrete events); after first, state update flushed before second click? In React 18, discrete events flush synchronously before yielding — so after first click, re-render + button disabled happens before the browser dispatches the second click of a double-click (double-click gap typically ≥ 50ms, plenty of time). Hmm — so is the double-click scenario actually feasible? Discrete input events flush sync, so between the two physical clicks of a double-click, React will have re-rendered and disabled the button... The second click targets the button element; if `disabled` attribute is set by then, the click event won't fire on it. So double-click protection via `disabled` is actually effective in React 18 for discrete clicks.

