Hmm, so is there any real path to duplicate cursors? What if the query for cursor N resolves from cache instantly (e.g., refetch after a previous session)? Then loadingMore may be false immediately... Scenario: user loads page 2, then filters change and back, or invalidation happens (apply success invalidates scope!). After apply-success invalidation, lists refetch; during refetch, isPending=false (isFetching true) — `results.slice(1).some(isPending)` false → button enabled while refetching. nextCursor = last answered page's nextCursor — the data is stale but present. If user clicks load-more while refetch in flight: appends nextCursor (say N) again — was N already in cursors? Yes, it's the last cursor — appending it AGAIN creates duplicate! Wait no: nextCursor is the NEXT cursor after the last page — not yet in the cursors array (unless already at end). Hmm: cursors [null, A, B]; pages answered: p1(next=A), p2(next=B), p3(next=null) → nextCursor null → button hidden. If p3 not yet loaded... cursors [null, A, B], answered p1..p3? cursors[2]=B loads p3. If p3 answered with next=C, nextCursor=C, click → [null,A,B,C]. No dup.

Duplicate requires appending the same next cursor twice in a row — requires nextCursor to remain C after already appending C, while loadingMore is false. loadingMore false when the query for C is not pending. The query for C was just created... unless it resolved instantly from cache! When would cursor C's query be cached? If the user previously paginated to C, then the pageKey changed (term/category) and changed back: cursors reset to [null] via derivation, but cache still holds [.., C] pages. User re-paginates: click load-more when nextCursor=A (cached!) — the observer for cursor A resolves from cache synchronously-ish (no pending). loadingMore false. nextCursor=B. Click → append B... this reconstructs the same array, no dup. Each click appends the next distinct cursor. Still no dup.

